{"name":"Downing Street","hex":"#4a4b57","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Downing Street","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A street leading off Whitehall in Westminster, London containing the residences of the Prime Minister and the Chancellor of the Exchequer","The British government."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Downing_Street"}],"entries":[{"title":"Downing Street","blocks":[{"text":"Downing Street is a gated street in Westminster in London that houses the official residences and offices of the Prime Minister of the United Kingdom and the Chancellor of the Exchequer in a cul-de-sac situated off Whitehall. It is 200 metres (660 ft) long, and a few minutes' walk from the Houses of Parliament. Downing Street was developed in the 1680s by Sir George Downing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Downing_Street_gates.jpg/330px-Downing_Street_gates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downing_Street"},{"title":"Street","blocks":[{"text":"A street is a public thoroughfare in a city, town or village, typically lined with buildings on one or both sides. Streets often include pavements (sidewalks), pedestrian crossings, and sometimes amenities like streetlights or benches. A street can be as simple as a level patch of dirt, but is more often paved with a hard, durable surface such as tarmac, concrete, cobblestone or brick. It can be designed for both social activity and movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Typical_Street_In_The_Royal_Borough_Of_Kensington_And_Chelsea_In_London.jpg/330px-Typical_Street_In_The_Royal_Borough_Of_Kensington_And_Chelsea_In_London.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Portrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Portrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Portrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Possible portrait of Sir George Downing by Thomas Smith, painted between c. 1675–1690","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_a_Man%2C_probably_Sir_George_Downing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/View_of_the_old_Foreign_Office_and_other_buildings_on_Downing_Street%2C_Westminster._1827.jpg/500px-View_of_the_old_Foreign_Office_and_other_buildings_on_Downing_Street%2C_Westminster._1827.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/View_of_the_old_Foreign_Office_and_other_buildings_on_Downing_Street%2C_Westminster._1827.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_old_Foreign_Office_and_other_buildings_on_Downing_Street%2C_Westminster._1827.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/View_of_the_old_Foreign_Office_and_other_buildings_on_Downing_Street%2C_Westminster._1827.jpg/500px-View_of_the_old_Foreign_Office_and_other_buildings_on_Downing_Street%2C_Westminster._1827.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"View of the old Foreign Office and other buildings on Downing Street in an 1827 watercolour by John Chessell Buckler","link":"https://en.wikipedi